;;;; Session table definition
;;;;
;;;; The table that the database session store keeps sessions in.  Create it
;;;; with:
;;;;
;;;;     clails db:create-session-table
;;;;
;;;; which reads THIS file, renders one CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS statement in
;;;; your database's dialect and runs it.  Running the command again does
;;;; nothing and keeps the rows that are already there.
;;;;
;;;; This is NOT a migration: it is data, read and never evaluated, and the
;;;; table it creates does not appear in the migration history or in
;;;; db/schema.lisp.  That is deliberate -- the session table is framework
;;;; bookkeeping rather than part of your schema -- and it is why
;;;; `clails db:migrate' and `clails db:status' are unaffected by it.
;;;;
;;;; To use the table, point the session store at it in
;;;; app/config/environment.lisp:
;;;;
;;;;     (setf clails/middleware/session-middleware:*session-store*
;;;;           (clails/middleware/session/store/db:make-db-session-store))
;;;;
;;;; The store reads the column names from this file, so renaming a column here
;;;; moves both halves at once.  Each column says what it is FOR with :role:
;;;;
;;;;   :id           the session id (40 characters, the primary key)
;;;;   :value        the serialised session
;;;;   :last-access  when the session was last used, which is what the idle
;;;;                 timeout is measured from.  It is a column of its own on
;;;;                 purpose: an updated_at column is not written unless
;;;;                 something writes it, and a session store that skips the
;;;;                 write when the session did not change would let the time
;;;;                 stand still for a user who is only reading pages -- and
;;;;                 expire them while they are using the application.
;;;;
;;;; Column keys: :name :role :type :length :not-null :primary-key :unique
;;;; :default (only :current-timestamp).  :type is one of the migration column
;;;; types (:string :text :integer :float :decimal :datetime :date :time
;;;; :boolean); the dialect's spelling of it (datetime against timestamp) is
;;;; chosen when the statement is rendered.

(:table "clails_sessions"
 :columns ((:name "session_id"
            :role :id
            :type :string
            :length 40
            :not-null t
            :primary-key t)
           (:name "session_value"
            :role :value
            :type :text
            :not-null t)
           (:name "last_access_at"
            :role :last-access
            :type :datetime
            :not-null t
            :default :current-timestamp)))
